Hello Hono team,

I found a small gap when trying to use `validator()` to parse a dot encoded FormData. Small example:
```html
<form>
   <input name="survey_id" />
   <input name="person.firstname" />
   <input name="person.lasname" />
</form>
```


Currently `validator` doesn't support dot notation at all [validator.ts](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/b68da3945e23f0ddef8878b08df3a359eeef4a75/src/validator/validator.ts#L108)

I'm having to do this manually:
```typescript
const body = structuredClone(await c.req.parseBody({ dot: true })) ?? {};
const form =
	await MyObjZodSchema.safeParseAsync(body);
if (form.success) {
    // form.data should be { survey_id?: string; person?: { firstname?: string; lastname?: string } }
}
```

It would be nice to support c.req.valid for these cases, but I don't want to break existing form users. Either way is fine, may I suggest `formbody` or `formparse` target?

Another thing to note is that Zod isn't able to parse the result of `c.req.parseBody`, so I used `structuredClone`. I'm assuming it's due to how the object is constructed (no prototype), so it needs to be cloned before going to Zod.

